<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="50676" href="schedule/2020/spring/CMN/529/50676.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2020" href="schedule/2020.xml">2020</calendarYear><term id="120201" href="schedule/2020/spring.xml">Spring 2020</term><subject id="CMN" href="schedule/2020/spring/CMN.xml">Communication</subject><course id="529" href="schedule/2020/spring/CMN/529.xml">Seminar Communication Theory</course></parents><sectionNumber>JL </sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Organizational Rhetoric</sectionTitle><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>This course will consider rhetoric in and of organizations as well as rhetorical methods in the study of organizational communication. We will review recent literature in organizational communication and management theory that uses rhetorical analysis; explore the connections between classical rhetoric and corporate and administrative rhetoric; and consider the role of rhetoric in organizational identification, identities, strategy, and ethics.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2020-01-21Z</startDate><endDate>2020-05-06Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>02:00 PM</start><end>04:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>M      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>4007</roomNumber><buildingName>Lincoln Hall</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Lammers" firstName="J">Lammers, J</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>